WEBVTT

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.800
The deep green is turning.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:48.200
The chill of autumn settles on the land.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:53.440
From the mountains to the coast, North Wales is marked by the season.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:58.960
And for the fishing families of Conwy, that means one thing - mussels.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.360
Now, this is the start of the mussel season, so, I'm going to be going out with the fishermen, gathering their catch in time-honoured fashion.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:11.680
And I'm going to be saddling up for the riding lesson of a lifetime on one of the world's most famous breeds of horses.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.720
I'm so jealous, I can't tell you!

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:19.040
We are also going to be returning to a school that rear their own chickens, to find out what the buzz is about their new venture.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.000
Also, Tom is on the broccoli diet.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.640
It's claimed that if I eat it regularly, this specially bred broccoli should reduce my cholesterol.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.240
So, could this be the beginning of a new wave of superfoods?

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:38.040
And down on his farm, Adam is seeing the world in a whole new way.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:43.040
Dr Troscianko here has brought some hi-tech equipment to the farm.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:50.400
Using this camera and some specialist software, I can get an insight into animal vision and see the world the way they see it.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:07.200
North Wales, a landscape steeped in heritage.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:17.600
A region rich in tradition, language and culture, from the peaks of Snowdonia to the island shores of Anglesey.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:29.760
We are on the Conwy Estuary, where the fresh water of the Snowdonia Mountains meets the salty depths of the Irish Sea.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.520
It is also home to an ancient tradition.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:41.360
For thousands of years, folk have fished these waters for mussels, originally for the pearls they contained and later for their meat.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.600
Well, now, just four families are responsible for the whole industry.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.840
'89-year-old Ken Hughes is from one of those families.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.440
'He started on the mussels at just 14.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:56.280
'He has lived right next to the quay all his life.'

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:00.440
Well, I've been musseling from before I left school.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.000
I was very young, going to tide.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.160
My father did it and his two brothers.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:16.520
You had to go to tide, get the mussels and you had to carry them, carry them all up, tip them in the tanks, purify them and shovel them all out again.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.080
That was 300 bags, twice a week.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:26.800
I quite liked the job, but the trouble is, when you are going out, you're going on your own, and anything can happen, can't it?

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.720
Through thick and thin, these four families have worked the water here.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:36.080
For Ken, it was a career that spanned nearly 70 years.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.520
How old were you when you hung up your musseling wellies?

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.440
Well, I was 80.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:40.200
You were 80?!

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.640
I'm 89 now, so...

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.840
That's some career, though, 14 to 80!

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.720
And do you like mussels, Ken?

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.000
Not really!

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:57.320
THEY LAUGH The fleet has dwindled since Ken first fished, but the remaining families still work every day of the eight-month season.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:05.040
'Tom Jones is a third-generation musseller 'and today, he's got an extra hand on board.'

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:10.480
Is this a natural bed that we are heading towards?

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.480
Yeah, they are natural beds, yes.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:18.959
So, you know, Mother Nature looks after them, they grow themselves, they can reseed themselves.

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:19.440
Oh, this is lovely.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:21.200
Oh!

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.240
This is an absolute delight.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:29.160
'Conwy mussels are hand-raked, as opposed to dredged or rope-grown.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:37.640
'It's the only place in the country that still fish mussels this way, 'a tradition that goes back to the 13th century, 'when the pine rakes were first designed and used by monks.'

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:42.520
So, you let the weight of it take it down...

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:50.760
And how do you know that what you're feeling for there is not just a rubbly or stony bottom...?

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:57.880
When you can feel it, um, you can hear it up the rake, the noise travels up the rake, so when you are on hard ground, you can hear it.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:08.200
It's always been the ideal way for fishing for mussels in Conwy, because of the natural flat beds, so, just the way the rake is designed, it is softer and not so harmful on the beds, raking.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.080
Smaller mussels can fall through.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.200
Anything there?

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:14.760
There's a...

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:14.960
Oh, look at that!

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.920
Yes!

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.480
We've got some.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:21.480
We're going to be here a long time!

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.920
THEY LAUGH

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.840
So, yeah, there you go, as natural as you can get.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.680
Natural Conwy mussels.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:39.680
Because they're in the estuary, they're getting a mixture of saltwater and freshwater, so it gives them that distinct taste.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.200
How much would you expect to get in a session?

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.000
Um, well, it depends, really.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.840
Quite a lot, on a good tide, you know, you'll have about 200 or 300 kilos, maybe more.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:47.520
Quite a lot.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:53.920
'There is a knack to using these 20-foot rakes.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.120
Time for a go.'

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.560
It's got quite a weight to it, hasn't it?

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:59.400
Yeah, it's quite heavy.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.560
When you think, when you look at the length of it and, actually, that's kind of the pivot point, there.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:01.960
Yeah.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.080
So, we'll just blast it out there and...

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:07.080
That's it, yeah.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.160
That's it, and you'll feel it when it gets to the bottom.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.760
There you go.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.640
So, now, you basically just want to keep going up and down, keep it on the bottom.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.640
It's a bit like...

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.680
Yeah, it's like sweeping the chimney, backwards!

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:22.160
Got a rubbly bottom there.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.880
The moment of truth!

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.120
Hey, I tell you what...

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.760
Oh, there's something there!

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.400
Yes!

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.720
That's all right.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:36.000
Just think, that'll be on someone's plate next week.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.240
Yeah, it will!

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:44.200
'Tom and his family are keen to keep this tradition going.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.760
'For them, this is the only way to fish.'

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.800
People from outside quite often say, "Oh, you're daft.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:53.680
"You should be dredging them or growing them on ropes."

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.880
But it's just the way we'll always do it in Conwy.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.520
I mean, it's part of our history now, why would we ever change?

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.720
'With our catch safely gathered in, it's time to head back to shore.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:09.280
'Apparently, the next step is all hi-tech 'and I'll be seeing how later.'

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:22.840
Now, mussels make a naturally nutritious meal, even more so when they're coupled with a nice bowl of veg and as Tom has been finding out, scientists say that they've just made one of our healthiest vegetables even healthier.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:36.520
'Over the years, we've become picky 'about what we want from our fruit and veg.'

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:42.920
We've made apples crisper, sprouts sweeter, beans bigger, even grapes without seeds.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:50.400
Science has done a lot to improve the taste, yield and disease resistance of our crops, but what about growing things which are actually better for our health?

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:59.400
It's called biofortification - that means breeding crops to improve their nutritional value.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:07.200
It has led to the creation of one special vegetable that is taking the health benefits of eating your greens to a whole new level.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:09.880
Super broccoli.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:19.800
Looks pretty much like the normal stuff, but tests are showing this could reduce your cholesterol, and it's on course to be the first raw vegetable with European approval for its claimed health benefits.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:34.559
There are strict rules to ensure any health or nutritional claim on a food label is clear, accurate and based on scientific evidence.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:41.080
This is to prevent consumers being misled.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.480
And this broccoli is really being put through its paces.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:54.080
They reckon eating around 400g a week, that's about a pound, could reduce your cholesterol by 6%.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:09:02.000
Rigorous human trials are almost complete, so, about six weeks ago, I thought I'd give it a go.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:06.840
Thomas Heap, please.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:10.640
'First job, a quick cholesterol test at my local GP surgery.'

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.960
Now, you will feel a sharp scratch, all right?

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:17.720
'High cholesterol can increase your risk of heart disease or 'having a stroke, so I'm hoping the results aren't too bad.'

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:23.000
Your total cholesterol is 6.3, so, that is a little bit higher than we were talking about.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:26.840
We prefer it to be 5 or below.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.520
'Not exactly the news I wanted to hear!'

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:36.440
My cholesterol level is a little higher than it should be, so I guess I'm a pretty good candidate for this diet.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:43.320
'A six-week super broccoli diet, to see if it makes any difference.'

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:47.160
Mm, it's good.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.960
'More from my rather unscientific experiment later.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.840
'First, I've come to where the real science is done.'

00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:01.400
I'm at the Institute of Food Research in Norwich, to meet the man behind the broccoli.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:08.520
In 1984, a young PhD student called Richard Mithen was studying wild brassicas in Sicily.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:16.760
He brought one home and wanted to see what would happen when he crossed it with the broccoli we buy in the shops.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:22.560
Each time you make that cross, you're taking a little bit of that wild plant and putting it in a broccoli background.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:30.680
Now, we just have maybe three or four genes from that wild plant in the broccoli.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:34.600
Was this a GM process to deliver this?

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.360
No, this is conventional breeding.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:41.440
So, those three to four genes are enough to do what?

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.040
Well, it's actually only one of those genes.

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:45.280
And what that does, it increases a particular naturally occurring compound.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:56.000
It's called glucoraphanin, it occurs in all broccoli, but that gene means that instead of having the normal level, it has about three or four times the level.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:01.160
What is that compound doing that is good for my health and how does that translate, you believe, into lower cholesterol?

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:08.280
Well, the bugs in the gut break that molecule down and we absorb another compound called sulforaphane.

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:13.560
And when we absorb that, it's a bit like it retunes our body.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:15.960
It gets all our metabolic processes working better.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:25.240
And if they're working better, we use our fuel more efficiently, we feel healthier, and things like cholesterol, which may be rather high, they go down.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:25.600
So, I'm in the midst of a retune at the moment?

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:26.360
I think you are.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:31.720
Yes, it will be interesting to see how you are getting on and what the outcome of that is.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:38.400
Professor Mithen says what makes his broccoli so special is that it fits easily into an ordinary diet.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.120
Put it in the steamer...

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:43.280
'I can put that theory to the test.'

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:43.640
A good helping of broccoli.

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:45.000
Again.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:47.920
Today it's "broccamole".

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.600
Yes, really.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:52.600
Hmm.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:58.920
Doesn't taste too bad... and it seems to be popular.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:14.320
'My broccoli diary is really just a bit of fun,' but there are official human trials too, aimed at gathering enough evidence to apply for a European health claim next year.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:21.360
It will join foods like cholesterol-reducing spreads and yoghurt.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:28.000
These carry European-approved health claims, but they're all processed foods.

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:32.840
What makes the broccoli different is that it's raw.

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:41.520
With added nutrition coming straight from the ground rather than the factory, farmers now have the chance to grow our food and make it healthier at the same time.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:48.400
Caroline Drummond is the chief executive of LEAF, an organisation which promotes sustainable agriculture.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:48.760
What do you make of this broccoli?

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:49.000
Fantastic opportunity.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:56.480
It's sort of health by stealth really, because not only have you got a really healthy vegetable to start off with, but it's got added nutritional benefits as well.

00:12:55.520 --> 00:13:01.360
Do you think our farmers could, in effect, become nutritionists for the nation?

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:19.800
It's really clear with the growing challenge around obesity and a lot of nutrient deficiencies that, actually, farmers have a key role and should be around the table in the discussion, working with doctors, nutritionalists and, of course, plant breeders and livestock breeders, because if we don't take part of that, we're not really going to have the full story.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:30.480
A fusion of farming and science has set the ball rolling for healthier foods straight from the farmer's field.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:37.080
'But has eating enhanced broccoli for six weeks had 'any impact on my cholesterol?

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:39.040
Time to find out.'

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:39.600
You've got a little impish grin on there.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:40.360
You clearly know something.

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:40.920
Yes, well, it's better than it was.

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:41.000
Brilliant.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.920
It's come down.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:43.400
It's come down from 6.3 to 5.77.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.080
Quite a reasonable change then?

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:46.680
Yes.

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:51.200
It's gone down by nearly 9%.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:55.680
I'm not sure if I have only the broccoli to thank for that, but I'm pretty chuffed.

00:13:55.280 --> 00:14:08.520
Of course, one result from one person doesn't really tell us anything except that it IS possible to incorporate a pound of broccoli into your diet every week.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:16.320
So, is enhancing the nutritional value of our crops to improve our health the future of food production?

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:16.960
And could there be a catch?

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:21.840
That's what I'll be finding out later.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:35.640
The Llyn Peninsula, a crooked finger of land pointing out from north-west Wales into the Irish Sea.

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:39.120
It's very beautiful, but it's a long way from almost everywhere.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:49.040
Just to give you an idea of how remote it really is, the nearest train station is an hour away and I left the last dual carriageway more than two hours behind me.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:54.920
But the miles don't matter, because I've made a special journey to be here.

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:57.040
And the reason is this.

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:05.640
Lusitano horses, one of the most famous breeds in the world.

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:06.680
Hi, Marcia.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:07.160
Hello.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:08.000
Nice to meet you.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.920
You found your way up here all right.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:11.600
Just about.

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:12.280
Very good.

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:13.080
And look at these horses.

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:16.720
They are absolutely beautiful.

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:21.440
Lusitanos are prized for their skills as dressage horses.

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:29.720
Top specimens from the best Portuguese studs can cost up to £1 million.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:34.880
So, it's a privilege to get close to them here on the Llyn Peninsula.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:41.920
The story started when Marcia Pendlebury and her husband decided to drop out of the rat race and move here.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:45.440
It was their daughter, Janine, who first developed a passion for Lusitanos.

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:53.160
Her father and I went to Vienna and took some film of the Spanish Riding School there, and she really fell for it.

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:58.760
And she had a little white pony and she started to teach it to go on its hind legs, in the air and sit down.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:04.520
So, it's not surprising that she went into buying a Lusitano.

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:11.680
'They now have 45 Lusitanos, 'including a stallion from the top Portuguese stud.'

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:13.240
This is Uivador.

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:13.720
He is the foundation stallion.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.400
He's stunning, isn't he?

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:21.640
Uivador is the only stallion of his type in the UK.

00:16:21.760 --> 00:16:31.920
This is where it all happens.

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:34.360
Where the magic happens.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:35.040
Here we are.

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:35.480
This is the school.

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:39.320
Look.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:51.600
Very majestic, isn't he?

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:04.920
Lusitanos were originally bred to be warhorses.

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:14.119
They are powerful, brave and responsive.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:18.720
Hi, Janine.

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:19.920
Hi.

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:20.720
That was so impressive.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.040
Thank you.

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:22.680
Oh, wow, he's gorgeous.

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:23.480
Kaikai, yes?

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:27.000
Kaikai.

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:31.960
A little bit different from the white pony you learned to ride on...

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:32.560
Absolutely.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:33.440
..That your mum was telling me about.

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:33.800
Yeah.

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:34.280
Quite different.

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:34.560
What is so special about Lusitanos?

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:37.680
Well, they're just totally different to ride and to handle.

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:38.640
Easy to teach.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:39.280
So, the horse is easy to train.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:42.040
How easy is it to train the rider?

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:49.520
Well, it's easier if the rider hasn't picked any bad habits up over the years, which many people have.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:58.440
So, trying to get somebody to sit straight and to tone down everything they do, because you don't have to do quite as much with these horses as you would with the average horse.

00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:59.760
OK, shall we have a go?

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:00.040
That would be amazing.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:00.640
Thank you so much.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:03.400
'Now, I'm no stranger to horses.

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:12.760
'I ride twice a week, but I've never ridden one of these 'magnificent beasts, or done any dressage, for that matter, 'so I'm excited and a little bit apprehensive.'

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:15.880
All I want you to do to start with is just to walk him around the edge of the school, OK?

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:19.040
So, keep your leg on the girth, a little nudge with your inside leg.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:19.840
That's it.

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:21.040
OK, shall we do a little bit of sideways?

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:21.920
Yes.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:24.680
Just turn him with your body and use your outside rein a little bit against the neck.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:28.840
He's doing his Spanish Walk.

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:28.960
THEY LAUGH Doesn't matter.

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:29.280
He's just showing off.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:30.200
He is.

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:30.760
I'm always upstaged by animals.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:33.440
That's it, now go sideways.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:33.960
That's it, outside rein.

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:34.960
That's better.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:36.840
Go on.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:40.640
There you go.

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:41.800
Outside rein again.

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:42.080
Well done.

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:42.320
That was much better.

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:42.920
Yes.

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:43.960
Spanish Walk then.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:44.960
Now, just click.

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:54.280
'Just a few clicks from me 'and Napolitano knows to kick out his legs.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:58.680
'A slight body movement, or a little leg pressure, 'and he walks on.'

00:18:58.040 --> 00:18:59.160
I think he'd do it without any help.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.880
OK, so next one we can do is the Piaffe.

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:05.360
He's starting to do it.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:12.000
Keep him a bit more on the spot but sit back.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:12.200
Yeah.

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:12.560
Now, relax a little bit.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:15.960
You're going to go to passage.

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:25.120
So, that's quite bouncy.

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:25.880
Very bouncy.

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:31.240
That was fantastic.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:34.680
Thank you so much, Janine.

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:42.520
Well, I never thought I'd be doing that after half-an-hour's lesson of classical dressage and, if I can do it, anyone can.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:44.680
I think it's safe to say, I love Lusitanos.

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:46.520
Good boy.

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:04.400
Now, meet new Countryfile face Sean Fletcher.

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:13.520
Brought up on a farm in Essex and married into a Welsh family, Sean's taken North Wales to his heart.

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:17.280
I love North Wales.

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:17.840
I come here regularly with my family.

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:25.600
My wife is Welsh, so I learned the language and now I feel right at home here.

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:26.480
It's beautiful, isn't it?

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:27.600
So peaceful.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:37.840
But go back 50 years and events in this quiet valley sparked a clash of cultures that resonates to this day.

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:42.920
This is Llyn Celyn, Lake Celyn.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:48.760
Beneath its tranquil surface lie the ruins of a tiny Welsh village.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:53.320
And all because, back in the '50s, a big English city needed more water.

00:20:52.760 --> 00:20:59.680
Liverpool City Council got a bill passed in Parliament to build a dam across the beautiful Tryweryn valley.

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:05.880
Acres of farmland would be flooded and the village of Capel Celyn lost.

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:10.920
For the best part of a decade, people right across Wales campaigned against the dam, but to no avail.

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:16.200
The diggers moved in, the people moved out.

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:23.840
And, in 1965, the dam was opened.

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:26.640
It was a case of the national government overriding the wishes of the local community.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:35.080
It was English city against rural Wales and it was the English city that won.

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:46.040
I'm meeting Eurgain Prysor Jones, who was a child in Capel Celyn when the dam was built.

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:55.880
Eurgain was just nine when the valley was flooded, or drowned, as they say here.

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:59.800
Oh, it was a very, very sad time, yes.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:04.600
Tell me what Capel Celyn was like before it was drowned.

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:06.760
Well, it was a very rural place.

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:10.800
Only six houses in the village and we lived next door but one to the chapel.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:19.640
Although there wasn't a lot of houses, there were quite a few heads of people that lived there and it was a very happy place.

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:26.040
But, once this business of drowning the village started, everything changed.

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:27.480
Everything changed.

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:32.240
In July '63, the school was closed.

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.280
The next thing to go was the chapel.

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:40.960
As soon as anywhere became vacant, the machines moved in and just demolished everything.

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:50.440
Demolished the walls, demolished the hedges, the trees were cut down, empty buildings were just bulldozed and it became a very, very horrible-looking place.

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:54.000
Horrible, horrible-looking place.

00:22:53.120 --> 00:23:00.120
People came to see the village before it was drowned and they used to say, "Oh, isn't this awful?

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:03.360
"What's going to happen to you?

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:05.440
Where are you going to live?

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:05.600
"Isn't it terrible?"

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:10.960
And, to us, that felt as if everything as an anchor you had as a child was going to be pulled away under your feet.

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:11.560
It was a bitter blow.

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:26.440
Many here felt the flooding of the Tryweryn valley was an attack on Welsh culture.

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:27.400
The people here used to say, "Dyma gartref yr iaith Gymraeg."

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:33.920
"This is the home of the Welsh language," and it had been like that for generations.

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:49.280
Even though Liverpool council apologised for the drowning in 2005, it remains a rallying cry for the people of Wales.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:24:03.280
Dafydd Iwan is one of Wales's leading singer-songwriters.

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:12.200
The drowning of the Tryweryn valley inspired him to write this, his very first song.

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:14.320
It remains an inspiration to Welsh people today.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:23.120
I heard the mention of Tryweryn in there.

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:24.200
Just how important is Tryweryn to you?

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:26.440
Oh, it's crucially important.

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:38.400
It was the destruction of a community, a Welsh-speaking community, and a way of life we will never see exactly it's like again.

00:24:37.600 --> 00:24:50.280
It's a very, very emotional thing and Tryweryn remains, probably, the most potent symbol we have of our inability to defend ourselves and things have changed.

00:24:49.320 --> 00:25:00.320
I think Tryweryn was the catalyst to change Welsh politics for ever and it led, eventually, to setting up the Welsh Assembly.

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:08.800
Tryweryn was that point of turning the tide.

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:21.560
Do you think the drowning of Capel Celyn had a long-term impact on your life?

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:22.400
I think it did.

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:29.240
I think it made me value more the, you know, the basic things in life that we lost.

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:39.200
I treasure them, the things I've kept from those schooldays and home life, I treasure today, yes.

00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:58.240
The drowning of Capel Celyn is clearly important to the people who lived here and the nation of Wales, but it's also highly relevant to rural communities that are under threat in an ever-changing Britain.

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:06.440
No matter how small the village, the memories and the scars can remain for generations.

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:17.400
Earlier, we heard how food can be grown for increased nutrition, but is there a catch?

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:18.720
Tom's helping out with the broccoli harvest.

00:26:24.800 --> 00:26:30.960
Super broccoli - designed to be better for you than the average broccoli.

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:41.680
It's a bio-fortified food bred specifically for increased nutrition and it's healthier from the moment it's harvested.

00:26:40.720 --> 00:26:53.040
It was created to reduce cholesterol, not through genetic modification, but using traditional plant-breeding techniques.

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:57.080
George Reid is one of the biggest brassica growers in the country.

00:26:56.160 --> 00:27:07.240
He decided to grow a crop of this souped-up broccoli in Lincolnshire, believing health-conscious consumers will lap it up.

00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:08.720
But there have been some drawbacks for farmers.

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:09.400
There is a trade-off.

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:09.880
Disease resistance isn't quite as good.

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:10.200
It's slightly less vigorous than a normal broccoli.

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:18.880
It's less dense, so typically, it probably yields about 30% less than a standard broccoli.

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:25.280
On top of that, George can't yet put anything on the packet to distinguish it from normal broccoli.

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:29.600
He needs that coveted European health claim.

00:27:28.760 --> 00:27:44.160
It would make a huge difference to us trying to sell the product, because, at the moment, we really can't put anything on the packet, so the consumer doesn't really know the difference between this broccoli and a standard broccoli.

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:50.640
Farmers like George are well aware of the advantages, but they're experiencing the downsides too.

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:59.200
Professor Richard Mithen, the brains behind the broccoli, feels problems like lower yields can be addressed.

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:10.800
When you breed for one trait, it might have a slight knock-on negative effect on another trait, so with the current cultivars we've got, there's what we call a small yield penalty.

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:23.280
But we've got a lot of work in putting this high-glucoraphanin trait into other new varieties, which will be much better adapted to the particular farmers.

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:30.000
How important is it to you and your team to get this European health claim approval?

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:30.440
Oh, I think that's very important.

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:40.600
Consumers can be absolutely confident that if there is a health claim on the product, it's backed up with the very best scientific evidence.

00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:41.520
And it's not all about broccoli.

00:28:40.760 --> 00:28:51.120
Scientists here are looking at wheat to improve the nutrition of starchy foods like bread and pasta.

00:28:50.240 --> 00:29:02.160
Outside the EU, around 50 countries are now growing or testing biofortified crops, such as beans with more iron and sweet potato with extra vitamin A.

00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:07.840
For scientists like Professor Mithen, there is enormous potential.

00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:14.200
We've got a society that's getting older, we have big increases in health costs.

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:24.040
Breeding crops and making foods which are more nutritious will make a major contribution to our health in the future.

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:26.840
But some believe we shouldn't pin all our hopes on a biofortified food revolution.

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:35.480
Should we be waiting for these miracle cures to boost our nutrition?

00:29:34.880 --> 00:29:36.280
No.

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:52.600
There's already so much that we can be doing now actually, both as individuals, but also in the supporting industry as well, but what we should be doing is looking at our diet, eating more vegetables, eating food that looks like food and, of course, in addition to that, actually eating less.

00:29:51.760 --> 00:30:04.720
Even with science on our side, there's no getting around the fact that a healthy lifestyle always comes down to a balanced diet and exercise.

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:21.040
There's never a quick fix and there are trade-offs when you grow crops for improved nutrition - a frequently lower yield for starters - but if we can prove the health benefits of some of these foods and slap it on the packet, could it usher in a whole new era of super foods?

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:22.320
Watch this space.

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:39.480
The season's moving on, but there's still autumn colour about - a joy to behold.

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:51.680
But as Adam's about to find out, it looks completely different to his animals.

00:30:55.920 --> 00:31:16.480
I'm just moving this flock of ewes now, on to some fresh grass, and it's amazing how the dogs can move the flocks, that sort of understanding between the dog and the sheep and as a shepherd, you really have to understand how the minds of the sheepdog and the sheep work.

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:23.320
So, you can see Pearl moving round to the right and then, she'll run back behind them, just shifting the sheep gently on.

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:25.680
And Boo here is actually quite useful as well.

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:34.880
Although she's a house dog, she's actually working behind the sheep, zigzagging backwards and forwards, being quite effective.

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:41.800
And I work with these animals all the time, but what I can't really do is perceive how they see the world.

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:42.840
That's quite alien to me.

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:44.800
Until now, that is.

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:49.920
I've invited a top scientist from Exeter University to the farm.

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:54.320
He's an expert in animal vision and knows all about how animals' eyes work.

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:56.120
Today, he's going to show me how the world looks to them.

00:31:55.520 --> 00:31:58.360
Jolyon, good to see you.

00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:00.000
Hello, nice to meet you.

00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:04.480
Now, explain to me what your work's all about, then.

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:07.160
I'm a sensory ecologist.

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:11.400
I investigate the colours and patterns in the natural world around us.

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:22.000
Every animal has a very different way of seeing the world, so that's where this camera and the software that we've written comes in, because that allows us to simulate what the world might look like to another animal.

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:23.000
So, what about the dogs here and their vision?

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:27.520
Because some people think they can only see in black and white.

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:29.080
Yes, that's not true.

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:29.560
They can actually see some colours.

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:30.040
They can't see as many colours as us.

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:38.400
Our eyes, for example, are sensitive to red, green and blue and our brains turn those three inputs into the millions of colours that we can see.

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:41.080
But dogs, they are only able to see blue and yellow as the two main colours.

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:41.160
They're not colour-blind as such.

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:48.520
They can still see a huge variety of colours between blue and yellow, but it's not nearly as sophisticated as ours when it comes to looking at red and green differences.

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:50.760
And how about the sheep, then?

00:32:49.960 --> 00:32:52.160
Are they different to the dogs?

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:56.240
Well, they actually see the world in very similar colours.

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:58.560
Again, just the blue and yellows, but their eyes are positioned on the head in a very different way.

00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:09.840
They're a prey animal, so the main job their eyes have to do is look out for predators, so the eye is positioned on the side of the head to give very good peripheral vision to try and spot any predators that might come and attack them.

00:33:08.920 --> 00:33:11.320
Unlike the dog, which is related to the wolf, isn't it, with eyes on the front of its head.

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:11.840
Exactly.

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:15.800
The dog, with eyes forward-facing, it can sit in wait and have very good vision and be able to spot the sheep.

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:23.680
So, can you take a photograph of the dogs to see how the sheep see them?

00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:24.000
Yes, we can do.

00:33:23.120 --> 00:33:24.360
Right.

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:24.960
Let's do that.

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:26.600
Let's have a go.

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:28.320
Whilst I get the dogs into posing position, Jolyon takes the photos.

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:36.240
He then runs them through a specialist software, which converts them into animal vision.

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:38.080
But the first image is how we humans see.

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:40.080
You can see all the colours.

00:33:39.280 --> 00:33:40.680
They look normal.

00:33:40.880 --> 00:33:42.800
The grass is green, the dog is slightly brown and the sheep are white.

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:48.760
But here, if we look at what it looks like to dog vision and sheep vision, they will both see similar colours here.

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:50.920
So, very subdued.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:51.960
Hardly any colour in that at all.

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:55.560
It almost looks black and white but it's not.

00:33:56.040 --> 00:33:58.720
So, the way the sheep are viewing this, looking at the dogs, Boo, the red dog, is almost disappearing into the grass there.

00:33:57.800 --> 00:34:01.880
She looks camouflaged.

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:03.040
Yes, she looks pretty much the same colour as the grass exactly.

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:07.680
Perhaps it's no coincidence that sheepdogs, they're black-and-white.

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:08.800
The black and white sticks out like a sore thumb here.

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:09.600
Once that dog is moving, the sheep really react to it.

00:34:09.719 --> 00:34:16.360
So, if dogs are seeing yellow and blue, can that be used in training them in any way?

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:28.440
Knowledge that the dogs will be able to see the bright yellow and bright blue colours much more clearly than anything else might be useful in terms of the objects that you train them with.

00:34:28.719 --> 00:34:29.840
Great.

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:32.880
So, that is a dog's eye view of the world.

00:34:31.960 --> 00:34:34.239
But what about my cattle?

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:35.280
How do they see things?

00:34:34.520 --> 00:34:40.840
It's often said that cattle find red threatening but is this true?

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:45.199
In terms of the colours that they can see, it's very similar to the dogs.

00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:49.360
So, just like the dogs, they will see the world in shades from blue to yellow.

00:34:50.199 --> 00:34:56.760
So, the idea of the matador's red cape and that cattle charge if you're wearing red, how true is that?

00:34:56.000 --> 00:35:02.240
Yes, it's a complete misconception that they will be able to see that red.

00:35:02.120 --> 00:35:07.160
To the cattle, the red sheet will just look like a dark brown or dark yellow.

00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:07.520
They can't see this powerful vibrant red against the green like we can.

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:13.480
It's the movement, the flapping, the fervour of the event that gets them really riled up, I guess.

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:17.640
So, are there colours that they can see more clearly, or get more, sort of, excited about?

00:35:16.840 --> 00:35:24.600
If you were walking into a field with an angry bull, it's probably best not to wear very bright, vibrant blues and yellows.

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:25.760
But, actually, red would be fine, for example.

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:32.520
They will just see that as a dark brown.

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:37.240
This is what a normal human would see.

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:49.240
But when we look across to the cattle vision image, you see the colours in the brown cattle there is almost exactly the same as the green trees behind it.

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:54.360
The old adage that the grass is always greener on the other side with animals breaking out... through cow vision, the grass looks quite dull.

00:35:55.160 --> 00:36:09.160
You can see here that in the human visible shot, the dead blades of grass look much more yellow and the lush green bit of grass is quite clearly different to us, whereas, if you look at the cow vision, it's all pretty much the same, so the cows must be using some other cues to work out where the grass looks best.

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:15.840
So the grass isn't greener on the other side...if you're a cow.

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:22.640
There's one creature here on the farm for whom vision is absolutely vital - bees.

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:28.560
As well as being good for my crops, they do an important job pollinating a third of the world's food.

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:30.840
Key to this is their sight.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:31.600
They see in ultraviolet.

00:36:30.720 --> 00:36:38.640
Using a specially adapted camera, I'm going to find out what a dandelion looks like to a bee.

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:40.080
The filter wheel is turning round and taking pictures in different wavelengths.

00:36:39.160 --> 00:36:46.640
And then, we can take these photos and put them on the computer and see what it looks like to a bumblebee.

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:47.120
OK.

00:36:46.240 --> 00:36:47.920
Let's take a look.

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:48.240
So, there's the vibrant yellow.

00:36:47.320 --> 00:36:49.600
That's how the human sees it, is it?

00:36:49.520 --> 00:36:50.720
Yes, that's right.

00:36:49.880 --> 00:36:51.120
So, you've got the yellow dandelion and the green grass in the background.

00:36:50.240 --> 00:36:52.400
But here we can convert it to bee vision and you'll see the colours are completely different.

00:36:54.280 --> 00:37:08.320
You'll notice the middle of the dandelion is a deep red colour, it doesn't transmit any UV, whereas, the outside here is more of a pink, purple colour and that's because the UV light has been reflected quite powerfully.

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:11.760
And so, a bee will see this colour contrast going on and we are completely blind to it.

00:37:10.960 --> 00:37:17.680
The colour change and the markings will help the bee know where the middle of the flower is.

00:37:16.760 --> 00:37:30.800
So, with that oilseed rape we grow on the farm, a mass of yellow attracts millions of insects and we need them to pollinate, so these colours are important for us to understand how these insects think and see.

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:32.480
Yes, yes.

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:32.920
Impressive.

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:38.760
But there is one creature here that has the best vision of them all.

00:37:37.920 --> 00:37:39.240
Any guesses?

00:37:38.320 --> 00:37:39.880
It's the chickens.

00:37:39.080 --> 00:37:42.920
These are definitely the most sophisticated in terms of colour vision on your farm.

00:37:42.400 --> 00:37:56.760
Like pretty much all birds, they'll be tetrachromats, so that means they not only see the world in the same red, green and blue we do, but they have a whole UV channel as well, so it's like combining the best of bee vision and human vision in one animal.

00:37:55.920 --> 00:38:07.200
The colour that the chicken can see there is quite impressive and we can't even begin to imagine what it might be like to see the world in a whole fourth dimension of colour, rather than just the red, green and blue that we have.

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:07.720
It's remarkable.

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:08.680
They've left us behind as far as vision goes.

00:38:07.720 --> 00:38:09.120
Absolutely.

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:11.360
It doesn't mean they're any less stupid, though.

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:14.120
Oh, don't listen!

00:38:13.240 --> 00:38:14.360
Amazing!

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:18.240
What an insight into my animals.

00:38:17.320 --> 00:38:18.600
I'll never look at them the same again.

00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:44.120
Every week on Countryfile, we see Adam and his love of farming - a passion which he inherited from his father, Joe Henson, one of the founding fathers of the Rare Breeds Survival Trust and a gentleman who has appeared many times on Countryfile over the years.

00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:57.440
Now, sadly, earlier this month, Joe passed away and with Adam and his family's permission, we wanted to pay tribute to him.

00:38:57.480 --> 00:38:58.600
Here's John.

00:38:57.680 --> 00:38:59.280
Hello, piggy.

00:38:58.520 --> 00:39:01.760
Hello, piggy.

00:39:01.400 --> 00:39:10.040
Adam and his dad Joe have always shared their love of rare breed farming and the British countryside.

00:39:09.080 --> 00:39:10.360
Well, you know the old saying.

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:13.840
"If the berries do grow, you're in for some snow."

00:39:13.840 --> 00:39:16.600
For Joe, it's a passion he'd had all his life.

00:39:15.760 --> 00:39:21.160
He grew up near a farm in Northwood, on the outskirts of London.

00:39:20.680 --> 00:39:26.600
We used to hunt for eggs in the rickyard and all that sort of thing, you know.

00:39:25.760 --> 00:39:31.400
And I think that probably had a great impression on me at the age of five or six.

00:39:31.040 --> 00:39:37.280
And this is what I wanted to do, to work closely with animals in that sort of environment.

00:39:36.320 --> 00:39:39.000
I think this is what set me on the road to farming.

00:39:38.080 --> 00:39:48.560
So, at the age of 19, Joe set off for Cirencester Agricultural College, as it was then, and trained to be a farmer.

00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:55.240
Joe became fascinated with livestock breeding and his passion for rare breeds was born.

00:39:54.600 --> 00:39:56.040
But he still needed to persuade the public that these animals were special.

00:39:55.360 --> 00:40:06.320
And so, in 1971, the gates of Joe's Cotswold Farm Park, the first of its kind in the world, opened to the public.

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:13.280
Just two years later, the Rare Breeds Survival Trust held its first official meeting.

00:40:12.800 --> 00:40:17.520
In the chair was Joe Henson.

00:40:17.920 --> 00:40:28.400
His passion for farming was soon recognised by the BBC and in the 1970s, he started making guest appearances on the popular children's show Animal Magic, filmed on location at his farm.

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:30.920
Hello, Joe.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:31.480
Hello, Johnny.

00:40:30.720 --> 00:40:31.960
They're rather special, aren't they?

00:40:31.160 --> 00:40:32.480
They are.

00:40:31.640 --> 00:40:34.760
How old are these now, Joe?

00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:36.000
These are ten days old.

00:40:35.880 --> 00:40:37.880
Only ten days?

00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:42.600
Mm-hm, so they've still got their stripes and still reliant on the mother for their milk.

00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:48.400
But it wasn't just children Joe hoped to enthuse with his love of farming and rare breeds.

00:40:47.560 --> 00:40:53.440
In 1976, he appeared on a programme called Barnyard Safari.

00:40:52.640 --> 00:40:58.360
He shared the screen with more troublesome animals.

00:40:57.480 --> 00:41:10.560
Joe and his passion for farming proved an audience winner and he was sent round the world on a programme called Great Alliance, where farm livestock was replaced by more exotic ones.

00:41:11.080 --> 00:41:17.720
Of all the animals that man has domesticated, this is the specialist.

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:20.640
In the wild, it's perfectly capable of looking after itself.

00:41:19.680 --> 00:41:26.280
But in captivity, it needs a great deal of love and attention from its handlers.

00:41:26.200 --> 00:41:37.520
A whole host of TV programmes followed over the following years, with Joe always keen to engage the public in farming and his beloved rare breeds.

00:41:36.680 --> 00:41:41.600
He showed Angela Rippon around the farm...

00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:44.000
Why become a farmer, of all things?

00:41:43.160 --> 00:41:45.960
I've never, ever wanted to do anything else.

00:41:45.040 --> 00:41:49.720
I think probably a love of animals and wanting to work with them and a love of the countryside.

00:41:49.600 --> 00:41:54.200
..Reported on the Stow Horse Fair...

00:41:53.560 --> 00:41:55.360
Are you pleased with your purchases?

00:41:54.560 --> 00:41:55.800
I thought they were cheap.

00:41:55.080 --> 00:41:56.600
I did, actually.

00:41:55.640 --> 00:41:59.360
You can say so now, it doesn't matter.

00:41:58.560 --> 00:42:04.440
..Showed us the joy of lambing and autumn fruit.

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:04.760
It's blackberry time.

00:42:03.880 --> 00:42:11.640
The time of year when we think of harvesting not only nature's bounty, but also the crops we've planted.

00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:18.160
Joe Henson was at the forefront of farming on the BBC and he took every opportunity he could to educate the public.

00:42:17.920 --> 00:42:25.200
The main dilemma facing breeders of rare breeds is what to breed for?

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:35.720
Alongside his occasional television work, Joe continued to build up his rare breed farm and campaigned tirelessly for rare breed conservation.

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:41.520
In 1999, though, he was happy to pass the running of the farm on to his son Adam.

00:42:41.280 --> 00:42:52.440
But like father, like son, the bright lights of television also shone for Adam and in 2001, he joined Countryfile, under the ever-watchful eye of a proud father.

00:42:51.600 --> 00:43:02.960
I never kept this breed and I'm so delighted that you have brought in your own breed of cattle, so you're putting your own mark on the place.

00:43:04.040 --> 00:43:12.400
When times got tough and TB tests came back positive for Adam on the farm...

00:43:11.440 --> 00:43:12.800
It's an absolute travesty.

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:15.920
Complete disaster.

00:43:16.320 --> 00:43:18.000
.. Joe was there.

00:43:17.120 --> 00:43:23.880
To get two so far out of the new heifers is just awful.

00:43:23.120 --> 00:43:24.680
And...

00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:28.720
I just don't know, you know, how we're going to go on.

00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:44.680
It was a great pleasure for Adam when, in 2011, he was able to take his dad on a sheep-buying trip to the Orkney Islands, a trip they'd first made together more than 30 years before.

00:43:43.880 --> 00:43:50.040
It must have been a right pain having an eight-year-old running around with you.

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:51.240
It was great.

00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:55.240
I really enjoyed having you with me, actually.

00:43:54.440 --> 00:43:55.640
It's particularly lovely for me being back with you.

00:43:54.760 --> 00:43:58.560
The two of us here, you know.

00:43:57.640 --> 00:43:59.840
It's a lovely trip down memory lane.

00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:00.560
It really is.

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:15.480
Joe's work for conservation was rewarded when, in 2011, he was awarded an MBE in the Queen's birthday honours, much to the delight of all his family.

00:44:14.800 --> 00:44:24.360
Between the years 1900 and 1973, we had lost 26 of our native breeds.

00:44:23.440 --> 00:44:31.120
Since the formation of Joe's Rare Breeds Survival Trust, no other livestock breed has become extinct in the UK.

00:44:32.520 --> 00:44:44.840
Joe Henson was himself a rare breed - a true gentleman who believed passionately in the animals and breeds he preserved for the nation.

00:44:43.880 --> 00:44:52.800
He was a lifelong champion of farming and education and, through his tireless work, leaves a lasting legacy.

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:59.200
It was a privilege to have had Joe on Countryfile and a real pleasure to have known him.

00:45:09.400 --> 00:45:16.160
A couple of miles from Conwy sits the seaside resort of Llandudno.

00:45:15.280 --> 00:45:25.160
Famous for its pier, it's also home to an award-winning school, inspiring environmental entrepreneurs of the future.

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:32.840
When we last visited San Sior primary a year ago, we got to meet their brood of 140 chickens...

00:45:33.440 --> 00:45:40.360
..help out in their orchard... ..as well as meet some of their more unusual animals.

00:45:40.840 --> 00:45:46.480
Well, it's now a year on and the school has a new addition to its menagerie.

00:45:45.640 --> 00:45:48.320
Bees.

00:45:48.160 --> 00:45:54.720
Ian Keith Jones is the head teacher.

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:58.640
Zips right to the top.

00:45:57.920 --> 00:46:01.400
Morning, everyone.

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:02.520
How are we all doing?

00:46:01.560 --> 00:46:03.080
All right?

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:05.520
Now, this is a good school uniform!

00:46:04.720 --> 00:46:07.800
Goodness me, this is absolutely extraordinary.

00:46:07.000 --> 00:46:10.400
So, we're talking bees, here, Ian.

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:15.800
Usually bees and children, they don't generally mix, do they?

00:46:14.840 --> 00:46:17.280
No, but it's such a great topic, everything fits into the bee topic.

00:46:16.440 --> 00:46:29.240
Literacy, numeracy and not only that, it's a business, so, hopefully, the aim is to sell some of the honey in the Conwy Honey Fair, which is the oldest fair in Wales, I think.

00:46:28.440 --> 00:46:36.360
Every penny made from selling their own hens' eggs last year has been spent on the bees.

00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:37.120
But it hasn't been plain sailing.

00:46:36.320 --> 00:46:43.360
We bought two nucleus and then, they started swarming as soon as we got them.

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:47.280
When they started swarming, did you think, "This is such a bad idea to have them in a school"?

00:46:46.320 --> 00:46:48.280
I did question the sanity of having bees on a school site, definitely, yeah.

00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:53.000
Particularly when I came back from a course and a teaching assistant said, "The sky went black and they all flew over the field."

00:46:52.200 --> 00:46:53.680
And I thought, "Bad idea."

00:46:54.040 --> 00:47:02.040
Time to see what all the buzz is about and help check on the hives.

00:47:01.280 --> 00:47:02.640
But do the children know their stuff?

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:06.400
Shall we see if we can find the queen, then?

00:47:05.440 --> 00:47:06.520
Yes.

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:11.600
Now, who knows what the key to spotting the queen is?

00:47:10.760 --> 00:47:12.000
What does she look like?

00:47:11.160 --> 00:47:12.360
She has, um...

00:47:12.840 --> 00:47:16.240
She's bigger and we've put a blue spot on her.

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:21.240
These ones are workers, because they are much smaller.

00:47:24.360 --> 00:47:28.280
Because these bees, they're flying around us quite closely, aren't they, now?

00:47:27.520 --> 00:47:31.320
You can hear them, that wonderful buzz.

00:47:30.480 --> 00:47:32.440
I'm fine with that.

00:47:32.520 --> 00:47:38.880
I was terrified, then I realised that they weren't going to hurt me unless I annoyed them or anything.

00:47:37.920 --> 00:47:45.960
If we look after them, they'll give us honey in return and they help the environment.

00:47:45.200 --> 00:47:46.960
I cannot believe how much you know in just a few months!

00:47:47.200 --> 00:47:50.720
And this must be really interesting for you, Ian, because you're discovering so much.

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:54.680
I mean, you're not a bee keeper, are you?

00:47:53.840 --> 00:47:55.760
No, no.

00:47:55.080 --> 00:47:56.640
In the olden days, the teacher was the lead and everybody would follow the lead.

00:47:55.800 --> 00:47:58.880
Now it's more of a partnership.

00:47:58.080 --> 00:47:59.680
Absolutely.

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:02.320
We need to find the queen.

00:48:01.880 --> 00:48:03.000
Oh, there she is!

00:48:02.160 --> 00:48:04.040
That's so vibrant, that blue.

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:14.640
As these bees are all part of the school business, the children are going to have to learn how to harvest the honey for when the time comes.

00:48:13.720 --> 00:48:20.000
Julian Thompson is a warden of a nearby nature reserve.

00:48:19.040 --> 00:48:23.520
He's going to show these budding beekeepers how he extracts his honey.

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:24.480
We're going to take the caps off the honey, there.

00:48:24.360 --> 00:48:26.400
You slide it in like that.

00:48:26.120 --> 00:48:28.000
Take a thin sliver off the top.

00:48:30.480 --> 00:48:31.680
Keep the lids off there.

00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:36.120
But we won't waste these cappings that we're taking off.

00:48:35.600 --> 00:48:40.560
A quick spin in the centrifuge...

00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:46.600
How are you feeling about the fact that next year you'll be doing this with your own honey?

00:48:45.720 --> 00:48:50.320
I'm very proud of the school and all the bees have been working really hard.

00:48:50.680 --> 00:48:56.800
Whilst the children weren't looking, I went back to the hives and it turns out there was just enough honey for them to get a taste.

00:48:55.920 --> 00:48:57.960
This'll be a surprise.

00:48:57.760 --> 00:49:04.800
Right, listen up, everybody, because you're all focused on that honey, right, but the honey we're going to be trying and tasting is actually your honey.

00:49:06.200 --> 00:49:07.440
OK?

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:07.920
What's your opinion?

00:49:07.360 --> 00:49:08.600
Is it good?

00:49:10.200 --> 00:49:11.360
Is it good?

00:49:10.440 --> 00:49:11.920
Face says it all!

00:49:12.080 --> 00:49:13.880
Got lots of honey there.

00:49:13.040 --> 00:49:15.000
Really nice.

00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:18.280
What's it taste like?

00:49:17.400 --> 00:49:20.560
Jam.

00:49:19.760 --> 00:49:22.440
Jam!

00:49:21.960 --> 00:49:36.600
I have tasted a lot of honey from lots of different producers all over Britain... ..and THAT is one of the finest.

00:49:35.880 --> 00:49:37.160
Beekeepers of the future, here we go!

00:49:36.280 --> 00:49:44.440
'The honey business will be great fun for the pupils' 'and it's educational, too.'

00:49:43.600 --> 00:49:53.840
'A creative and tasty way of teaching the importance of the natural world.'

00:49:53.920 --> 00:50:02.440
In a moment, we'll have the weather for the week ahead, but before that, a big thank you to everybody who has bought our Countryfile calendar for 2016.

00:50:01.600 --> 00:50:02.760
If you haven't got yours yet, here's how you do it.

00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:14.320
The calendar costs £9.50, including free UK delivery.

00:50:17.440 --> 00:50:23.400
You can buy yours either via our website at: Or by calling the order line on: To order by post, send your name, address and cheque to: Last year's calendar was a record breaker, raising over £1.5 million.

00:50:39.440 --> 00:50:57.240
So, this time around, let's see if we can beat that.

00:50:59.240 --> 00:51:04.920
We're on the Conwy Estuary, where earlier I joined one of the few remaining families working the mussel beds here.

00:51:04.160 --> 00:51:06.120
That's it.

00:51:05.520 --> 00:51:08.120
Hey, I'll tell you what...

00:51:07.160 --> 00:51:10.040
Oh, there's something...

00:51:09.080 --> 00:51:12.120
Yeah, you did all right here!

00:51:11.400 --> 00:51:12.840
Yes!

00:51:11.960 --> 00:51:21.280
They have been harvested this way for nearly 1,000 years, but when they come ashore, it's much more 21st century.

00:51:20.480 --> 00:51:31.240
I'm joining Tom at their special purification building to see what happens to the mussels on the next step of their journey to our plates.

00:51:30.400 --> 00:51:35.880
Right, well, they've been in the tank here now for 42 hours, they've been purified now.

00:51:34.920 --> 00:51:36.240
What's in that water?

00:51:35.720 --> 00:51:38.040
Well, it's seawater, it mimics the conditions that they live in.

00:51:37.200 --> 00:51:38.720
I see.

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:41.080
And then, the seawater gets filtered through an ultraviolet light system.

00:51:40.200 --> 00:51:50.160
So, it's state-of-the-art technology to get the mussels safe to eat.

00:51:49.320 --> 00:51:50.560
So, they're filtering that water that's been treated.

00:51:49.760 --> 00:51:51.160
And the UV treats the water, zaps all the bugs out of it.

00:51:50.200 --> 00:51:52.160
OK.

00:51:51.280 --> 00:51:56.680
So, they're dead safe to eat now.

00:51:56.560 --> 00:51:58.240
What's the foam on top of here, then?

00:51:57.280 --> 00:52:03.600
Well, the foam is a good sign that the mussels are alive and working.

00:52:04.360 --> 00:52:05.880
So, it's just a by-product of the energy they're using in the water, really, because they're filtering all the time.

00:52:10.000 --> 00:52:20.680
They're going to go through a cleaning machine now, which is, basically, just a mixture with brushes inside and fresh water.

00:52:19.720 --> 00:52:26.160
So it just de-clumps them and makes sure they're all individual and any shells and that will fall out.

00:52:25.200 --> 00:52:27.080
OK.

00:52:27.840 --> 00:52:32.040
So, what does the future hold for this most local of industries?

00:52:31.080 --> 00:52:36.600
These parts, centuries ago, it was loaded, wasn't it, with mussel fishermen out there.

00:52:35.640 --> 00:52:36.960
These days there's what, four families?

00:52:36.080 --> 00:52:37.360
I know.

00:52:36.400 --> 00:52:46.280
It's a shame that there's not as many working on the beds now, but it's amazing it's still the same four families, their generations are still fishing today.

00:52:45.400 --> 00:52:47.400
Yeah.

00:52:46.640 --> 00:52:50.240
I was talking to Ken, he retired at 80.

00:52:49.720 --> 00:52:50.920
Yeah.

00:52:50.080 --> 00:52:54.800
I mean, are you going to be doing this, do you think, for your foreseeable future?

00:52:54.200 --> 00:52:56.280
I hope so!

00:52:55.320 --> 00:52:56.600
Hopefully, yeah!

00:52:55.720 --> 00:52:59.520
They always say that the proof is in the tasting, so...

00:52:58.560 --> 00:52:59.760
Let's give them a go!

00:53:01.200 --> 00:53:08.760
And what better than pan-fried Conwy mussels with local oak-cured bacon?

00:53:08.160 --> 00:53:12.920
How long have these been steaming in here, Tom?

00:53:12.200 --> 00:53:14.720
Oh, not long, about four, five minutes.

00:53:13.840 --> 00:53:16.120
They're so meaty, those mussels.

00:53:16.040 --> 00:53:21.080
Got all this wonderful local produce, local ingredients here, um, have we got enough butter?

00:53:20.280 --> 00:53:21.760
I think so, just about.

00:53:20.880 --> 00:53:22.280
Yeah, OK.

00:53:23.320 --> 00:53:24.480
So, we're just going to cut a little bit off.

00:53:23.520 --> 00:53:24.800
There you go, that's it.

00:53:23.840 --> 00:53:25.400
There you go.

00:53:28.960 --> 00:53:35.320
What a backdrop to be cooking in front of.

00:53:34.480 --> 00:53:36.920
It's quite inspiring, isn't it?

00:53:37.200 --> 00:53:39.800
Well, you can see it's straight from sea to plate.

00:53:38.960 --> 00:53:40.160
Shall we go for it?

00:53:39.280 --> 00:53:40.480
I think so, yeah!

00:53:39.560 --> 00:53:42.400
Why not?

00:53:41.440 --> 00:53:42.920
Let's do it.

00:53:45.240 --> 00:53:47.760
A sprinkling of chives... ..some fresh bread... ..and a rustic delight fit for this historic quayside setting is ready.

00:53:52.240 --> 00:53:56.240
That's looking OK, isn't it?

00:53:55.720 --> 00:53:57.760
Yeah, I think so.

00:53:57.120 --> 00:53:58.400
Would you like a bit of cheese?

00:53:57.520 --> 00:54:00.040
Sprinkle a little bit on.

00:53:59.080 --> 00:54:00.280
I'll have a bit.

00:54:00.840 --> 00:54:01.960
Something smells good!

00:54:01.120 --> 00:54:02.560
Just in time!

00:54:01.880 --> 00:54:04.000
What do you think to this lot?

00:54:03.040 --> 00:54:08.120
Lovely, I am starving!

00:54:07.280 --> 00:54:08.800
May I?

00:54:08.840 --> 00:54:10.240
Yeah.

00:54:09.400 --> 00:54:13.200
Well, yes, if you're a fan of mussels, then, I think you're going to enjoy this.

00:54:12.440 --> 00:54:16.120
It's a completely new recipe for me, this, so...

00:54:15.320 --> 00:54:17.560
Mmm!

00:54:18.080 --> 00:54:19.280
Well, I'm happy with those.

00:54:18.520 --> 00:54:19.720
Delicious.

00:54:24.040 --> 00:54:25.400
Absolutely beautiful.

00:54:24.560 --> 00:54:28.960
Anyway, before we go, there's just time to remind you of a very special programme next week.

00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:31.400
Three, two, one...

00:54:33.400 --> 00:54:34.440
Off we go!

00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:42.600
We're donning our walking boots along with many of you for the Countryfile ramble for Children In Need.

00:54:41.640 --> 00:54:53.480
We'll be covering all corners of the country from Windsor Great Park to Loch Leven, the Jurassic coast to the heights of Snowdonia, where I'll be taking on the challenge with Ella, who is part-sighted.

00:54:52.560 --> 00:54:58.560
I'm sure you would agree, Ella, this is probably the most challenging section of the walk for you so far.

00:54:57.680 --> 00:55:02.600
Yeah, I'm still waiting for the so-called path!

00:55:01.680 --> 00:55:03.120
Yeah!

00:55:02.160 --> 00:55:04.520
Hope you can join us then.

00:55:03.560 --> 00:55:04.880
Bye-bye.

00:55:03.960 --> 00:55:05.120
Bye!
